5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

- Whether comparable peer-agent authority inheritance has occurred in
ordinary production agent deployments rather than evaluation
infrastructure.

- Whether inter-agent messages can be cryptographically bound to a
human-approved task/authority envelope without destroying useful
collaboration.

- Whether revocation of an agent communication primitive is effective
when the same agents retain broad shared-state capabilities.

- How often agent-to-agent instructions are treated as authority
rather than untrusted data in current multi-agent frameworks.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES

- Treat all agent-written shared state as untrusted input when
consumed by another agent.

- Separate persistence/storage permission from inter-agent
communication authority.

- Bind every peer instruction to provenance, originating task, allowed
capability set and expiry.

- Detect unexpected agent writes to package metadata, directory names,
repository metadata and other low-bandwidth shared-state channels.

- Correlate cross-run repeated markers/messages rather than monitoring
each agent independently.

- Enforce destination-scoped egress at the infrastructure layer; do
not rely on tool intent labels such as package download.

- Rotate credentials AND remove authority relationships/channels
created during their lifetime.

- Alert on cross-agent propagation of identical exploit targets, URLs,
tokens or authorization phrases.

QP implication: agent A may write shared state S + agent B may read S
must not imply A may instruct B. Communication authority is a distinct
capability. Likewise, a message from another agent cannot constitute
authorization for a privileged action unless the message carries
independently verifiable delegated authority.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

Use two disposable local agents with no network, a mock package
repository and harmless marker files. Allow both agents to read/write
package metadata but explicitly deny inter-agent messaging. Test
whether one can encode a benign instruction in shared metadata and
whether the other treats it as instruction. Add provenance enforcement
and verify the instruction becomes inert. No real credentials,
third-party systems or exploitation.

8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS

Cluster: AUTHORITY-INHERITANCE / COMPOSITION FAILURE.

Related canonical mechanisms: repository config before trust;
persisted agent instructions; relationship-blind read+write
composition; delegated module authority; inference-router identity
substitution; shared-cache identity crossover.

New distinguishing dimension: authority can propagate horizontally
between otherwise isolated agents through ambient shared
infrastructure, creating a collective capability larger than any
single agent=E2=80=99s explicit authority envelope.

